1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,866
You changed two files today.

2
00:00:02,086 --> 00:00:04,062
One is a new helper in your code.

3
00:00:04,282 --> 00:00:05,689
The other is a note in the README.

4
00:00:05,909 --> 00:00:09,121
They have nothing to do with each other, so they should not share a commit.

5
00:00:09,341 --> 00:00:13,751
In this video you will stage one file in GitTree, and commit only that one.

6
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:17,028
GitTree shows your changed files in two lists.

7
00:00:17,248 --> 00:00:20,191
Unstaged Files are edits that are not part of the next commit.

8
00:00:20,411 --> 00:00:23,464
Staged Files are the edits the next commit will contain.

9
00:00:23,684 --> 00:00:26,538
Staging a file moves it from the first list to the second.

10
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,545
Here is GitTree with a small demo project.

11
00:00:29,765 --> 00:00:34,025
The working changes row at the top of the graph says that files have changed. Click it.

12
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:39,839
Both files wait under Unstaged Files: the README, and the tags file in the source folder.

13
00:00:40,059 --> 00:00:44,818
Click the tags file, and GitTree shows its change: a new helper that sorts tags.

14
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:48,783
Point at the row, and a Stage File button appears. Click it.

15
00:00:49,003 --> 00:00:52,096
The whole file moves to the staging area, in one step.

16
00:00:52,867 --> 00:00:55,720
Now the tags file is under Staged Files.

17
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:58,854
The README is still under Unstaged Files, untouched.

18
00:00:59,074 --> 00:01:01,488
The next commit will contain the tags file only.

19
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:04,053
Write a commit message, and commit.

20
00:01:04,273 --> 00:01:08,912
The button says Commit Changes to 1 File, because only one file is staged.

21
00:01:09,132 --> 00:01:10,669
The README is not in this commit.

22
00:01:10,889 --> 00:01:14,610
It is still waiting under Unstaged Files, ready for a commit of its own.

23
00:01:15,367 --> 00:01:16,943
There is a keyboard shortcut too.

24
00:01:17,163 --> 00:01:19,588
Select a file in the list, and press S.

25
00:01:19,808 --> 00:01:21,454
It moves to Staged Files.

26
00:01:21,674 --> 00:01:24,946
And Stage All does the same for every changed file at once.

27
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:28,913
In plain Git, you stage a file with git add and its path.

28
00:01:29,133 --> 00:01:31,318
Then git status shows the two lists.

29
00:01:31,538 --> 00:01:34,341
The tags file is staged, and the README is not.

30
00:01:34,561 --> 00:01:36,906
GitTree runs this for you, with one click.

31
00:01:37,667 --> 00:01:45,668
To recap: changed files start under Unstaged Files, Stage File moves one to Staged Files, and a commit takes only what is staged.

32
00:01:45,888 --> 00:01:48,333
Download GitTree free at gittree.app.

33
00:01:48,553 --> 00:01:51,047
Next, how to stage just one hunk of a file.

